<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guido Tarlati</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1327">1327</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guido Tarlati was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> from 1312 and lord of the city from 1321 until his death in 1327, leading the Ghibelline party in Tuscany. He expanded Arezzo's territory, rebuilt its walls and crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis the Bavarian</a> King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1327, despite papal excommunication. His tomb in Arezzo cathedral, made by Agostino di Giovanni and Agnolo di Ventura, records his deeds.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guido-tarlati.jpg" alt="Guido Tarlati" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vincenzo Desiderio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CenotafioGuidoTarlatiparticolare.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
